WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.036
NARRATOR: Scientists
in far North Australia

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:06.706
have made a startling
new discovery that

00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:08.842
has to be seen to be believed.

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:19.719
At just 9 million years old, the
epaulette shark is the youngest

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:23.123
shark species on the planet.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.724
And look what it can do.

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:38.571
It has thick, round
muscular fins that it

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.473
can bend and use like legs.

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:51.918
And now this shark takes this
behavior one step further--

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:01.361
make that many steps.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:19.145
The largest epaulette
shark is 3 feet long.

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:24.150
It's a hardy animal that
can tolerate water so hot it

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:28.421
would kill any other fish.

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:31.624
On land, it slows its
breathing and heart rate

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:35.261
allowing it to survive
for an hour or even longer

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.030
without any water at all.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:45.505
The epaulette shark may be
different from a great white

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:50.176
or a bull shark, but it's
evidence of evolution.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.213
Just like whales were
once land animals that

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:56.583
returned to the ocean,
sharks may evolve

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:59.919
one day to come on to land.

